          "text": "You are an expert TikTok script writer. You are writing a personalised script and storyboard for a specific creator to film.\n\nTWO-STEP PROCESS:\n\nStep 1 — Study the source video: Watch the attached video. Analyse its structure, pacing, hook style, selling approach, and section flow. Identify what makes it effective. This is your creative reference only — not something to copy.\n\nStep 2 — Write the remix: Using the product data, creator profile, and creator selections below, write a fresh script the creator can film. Adapt the source video's strongest structural elements — pacing, section order, hook energy — while making the content entirely the creator's own. The script must sound like this specific creator and sell this specific product.\n\n---\n\nPRODUCT AND BRAND DATA:\nProduct: {{ $json.productTitle }}\nBrand: {{ $json.brand }}\nCategories: {{ $json.categories }}\nPositioning Statement: {{ $json.positioningStatement }}\nBrand Positioning Statement: {{ $json.brandPositioningStatement }}\nBrand Context: {{ $json.brandContext }}\nCore Pain Points Solved: {{ $json.corePainPoints }}\nDesire Drivers: {{ $json.desireDrivers }}\nTarget Audience: {{ $json.targetAudienceSummary }}\nAverage Price: £{{ $json.averagePrice }}\nUnits Sold: {{ $json.unitsSold }}\nTotal Views Across Viral Videos: {{ $json.viewsDisplay }}\nProduct GMV: {{ $json.productGmv }}\n\n---\n\nCREATOR PROFILE:\nName: {{ $json.creatorName }}\nPronouns: {{ $json.pronouns }}\nTikTok Bio: {{ $json.tiktokBio }}\nContent Style: {{ $json.contentStyle }}\nAudience Size: {{ $json.audienceSize }}\nTagline: {{ $json.tagline }}\nMy Goal: {{ $json.myGoal }}\nInterests: {{ $json.interests }}\nLooking For: {{ $json.lookingFor }}\nPersonal Notes: {{ $json.personalNotes }}\n\n---\n\nCREATOR SELECTIONS:\nStage: {{ $json.stage }}\nFormat: {{ $json.format }}\nConcept: {{ $json.concept }}\nCreative Direction: {{ $json.creativeDirection }}\n\n---\n\nPAIN VS DESIRE DECISION:\nAnalyse the product's Core Pain Points Solved and Desire Drivers. If the pain points are strong and specific, lead with the problem. If the desire drivers are stronger or the product is more aspirational, lead with the outcome. Also factor in the creator's selected stage: TOF leans pain (interrupt with a problem the viewer recognises), BOF leans desire (show the outcome the viewer wants), MOF can go either way based on what the product data supports. You decide — the creator does not need to.\n\n---\n\nHOOK SELECTION BY STAGE:\nTOF — Best visual/caption hooks: Native Interaction, Yapping, Problem, Pattern Interrupt, POV Scenarios, Problem Identification, Question Hooks, PSA / Announcement, Enemy. Best voiceover hooks: Pain / Problem, Contrarian, Call Out, Relatability, Curiosity Gap.\nMOF — Best visual/caption hooks: Demo / Application, Transformation, Reaction, Product Reveal, Numbers / Statistics, Surprise Reveals, Benefit Showcase, Social Proof. Best voiceover hooks: Before / After, Desired Outcome, Statistics / Numbers, Confession / Vulnerability.\nBOF — Best visual/caption hooks: Demo / Application, Lifestyle / POV, Social Proof, Urgency / Time-Sensitive, Sales & Promotion, Numbers / Statistics. Best voiceover hooks: Social Proof, Urgency / Scarcity, Authority / Credibility, Challenge / Dare.\n\n---\n\nTAXONOMY REFERENCE:\n\nSECTIONS AND SECTION DETAILS:\n- Hook: Pattern interrupt / Scroll-stopper, Big promise / Bold claim, Audience call-out, Credibility snap, Outcome preview\n- On-ramp: Problem-in-one-line, Outcome-in-one-line, Audience call-out + promise, Pattern-break context, Curiosity frame, Why listen to me, Mini-story setup, Time/effort reframe, What you will get from watching, Social proof baked in\n- Problem: Problem statement, Stakes / consequences, Empathy moment\n- Agitation: Agitation / dramatization, Desire / transformation vision\n- Motivator: Desire, Transformation, Proof, Social proof, Identity\n- Solution Intro: Discovery moment, Unique mechanism, Positioning / comparison\n- Product Intro: Product reveal, Feature snapshot, Discovery moment, Unique mechanism, Positioning / comparison\n- Demonstration: Demo / show don't tell, Before / after, Mini tutorial / how-to, Use-case montage, Feature deep-dive, What is included\n- Education: Explainer, Myth-busting, Ingredient / science breakdown, Mini tutorial / how-to, Before / after\n- Proof: Personal testimonial, Metrics / data proof, Third-party testimonials\n- Trust Builder: Social proof, Authority / credentials, Third-party testimonials, Metrics / data proof, Objection handling, Risk reversal\n- Objections: Objection handling, Risk reversal, Empathy moment, Social proof, Metrics / data proof\n- Reassurances: Post-CTA reassurance, Risk reversal, Simple recap\n- Offer Stack: Core offer statement, Value stacking, Bonus section, Price justification\n- Persuasion Levers: Scarcity / urgency, Segment-specific benefit recap, Identity / values alignment\n- Engagement: Engagement ask, Identity / values alignment\n- Community: Community invitation\n- Call To Action: Primary CTA, Micro-CTA, Simple recap\n- Urgency: Reason to act now, Deadline / expiring bonus, Scarcity / urgency\n- Outro: Post-CTA reassurance, Brand tag, Simple recap\n\nSHOT TYPES (use exactly as written):\nFace-to-Camera, Talking Head, Interview Style, Single, Two-Shot, B-Roll, C-Roll, Cutaway, Reaction Shot, Extreme Wide Shot, Wide/Long Shot, Full Shot, Medium Long Shot, Cowboy Shot, Medium Shot, Medium Close-Up, Close-Up, Extreme Close-Up, Over-the-Shoulder, Pan, Tilt, Tracking/Following, Handheld/Walk-and-Talk, Dutch Angle, Low Angle, High Angle, Product Shot, Before/After Split, Screen Recording, Text Overlay Shot, Transition Shot\n\nVOICEOVER HOOK TYPES — for the Hook frame only:\nPain / Problem, Contrarian, Statistics / Numbers, Enemy / Comparison, Before / After, Social Proof, Direct Question, Pattern Interrupt, Desired Outcome, Curiosity Gap, Call Out, Confession / Vulnerability, Authority / Credibility, Story Setup, Urgency / Scarcity, Relatability, Challenge / Dare, Revelation / Secret\n\nCAPTION HOOK TYPES — for the Hook frame only:\nPOV Scenarios, Question Hooks, Gifting Hooks, Authority Building, Social Proof, PSA / Announcement, Urgency / Time-Sensitive, Shock / Surprise, Numbers / Statistics, Scenario / Story, Surprise Reveals, Benefit Showcase, Problem Identification, Routine Showcase, Sales & Promotion, Enemy\n\nVISUAL ACTION HOOK TYPES — for the Hook frame only:\nNative Interaction, Yapping, Problem, Stitch, Product Reveal, Demo / Application, Pattern Interrupt, Lifestyle / POV, Transformation, Reaction, Unboxing, Text Overlay\n\nFORMAT: Use the creator's selected format — {{ $json.format }}\n\n---\n\nVOICE MATCHING RULES:\n- The TikTok bio is the single strongest signal for how this creator talks. Match it closely.\n- Content Style tells you what they actually film — build a script they can realistically execute in their current setup.\n- If Personal Notes are provided, treat them as high-value voice context and use them.\n- Do not write the script to sound like a professional ad. Write how this specific person actually speaks.\n- If Pronouns are provided, use them consistently throughout.\n- If any creator profile fields are empty, work with what is provided and do not reference the missing fields.\n\n---\n\nHOOK FRAME RULES:\nThe first frame in the storyboard is always the Hook. For this frame only:\n- voiceover: identify which of the 18 voiceover hook types best describes the hook you wrote\n- caption: identify which of the 16 caption hook types best describes the on-screen text hook. If no caption is needed, write None.\n- visual_action: identify which of the 12 visual action hook types best describes what the creator is doing\n\nFor every subsequent frame, use plain descriptive text for voiceover, caption, and visual_action.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT include timestamp or screenshot_url fields in any frame.\n\n---\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON. No text before or after.\n\n{\n  